  </section>

  <section class="sec" style="padding-top:36px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <article class="article">
        <p class="lede">A virtual assistant can cost anywhere from $6 an hour to $60 an hour, and both ends of that range can be the wrong buy. The price depends less on the person and more on the hiring model you choose. This guide breaks down what each model actually costs in 2026, what drives the number, and which costs never show up in the quote.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <p><strong>The short answer:</strong> freelance virtual assistants typically charge $10 to $30 an hour, US-based agencies charge $35 to $60 an hour, and a dedicated full-time remote professional through a staffing partner usually runs $1,500 to $2,500 a month all in. The cheapest hourly rate is rarely the cheapest outcome once recruiting, turnover, and management time are counted.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>The three ways to hire, and what each one costs</h2>
        <p>Every virtual assistant arrangement falls into one of three models. They look similar on a job board and behave very differently in practice.</p>

        <h3>1. Freelance marketplaces</h3>
        <p>Platforms like Upwork and Fiverr list virtual assistants from about $5 to $40 an hour, with a median around $13 to $15 an hour for general admin work. You handle everything yourself: writing the post, screening applicants, testing skills, managing the contract, and replacing the person when they disappear or take another client. The rate is low because all of that risk and work sits with you.</p>

        <h3>2. Hourly agencies</h3>
        <p>US-based virtual assistant agencies typically charge $35 to $60 an hour, often in prepaid monthly hour blocks. The agency recruits and employs the assistant, and you manage the day-to-day work. It is a solid model for light, variable workloads, but the hourly math gets expensive fast: 30 hours a week at $40 an hour is roughly $5,200 a month for part-time coverage.</p>

        <h3>3. A dedicated remote professional</h3>
        <p>A staffing partner recruits a full-time professional who works only for your business, on your hours, inside your tools. At Remote Team Partners this is one monthly rate per professional, scoped to the role and quoted exactly on a discovery call. Recruitment, employment, payroll, and compliance are included, and the person is dedicated to you rather than split across clients. For a full-time role this model usually lands 40 to 60 percent below the loaded cost of a US hire, a comparison we break down in our <a href="../../blog/remote-team-vs-in-house-cost/">remote team vs in-house cost guide</a>.</p>

        <h2>Virtual assistant cost by role</h2>
        <p>Role and seniority move the price more than geography does. Typical 2026 market ranges for a full-time dedicated professional look like this:</p>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Role</th><th>Freelance hourly</th><th>Dedicated monthly (all in)</th></tr>
          <tr><td>General administrative assistant</td><td>$8 to $20</td><td>$1,200 to $1,800</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Executive assistant</td><td>$18 to $40</td><td>$1,800 to $2,800</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Customer support representative</td><td>$8 to $18</td><td>$1,200 to $2,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bookkeeper</td><td>$20 to $45</td><td>$1,800 to $3,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Marketing or sales support</td><td>$15 to $35</td><td>$1,800 to $3,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Specialist or technical roles</td><td>$30 to $75+</td><td>$3,000 to $5,000+</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p>These ranges assume vetted, English-proficient professionals working full-time hours. Rates far below them usually signal an unvetted hire with no replacement guarantee, which becomes expensive the first time the person quits mid-project.</p>

        <h2>What actually drives the price</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Experience level.</strong> A first-year assistant and a five-year operations professional are different products. Seniority is the single biggest price driver.</li>
          <li><strong>Specialization.</strong> Bookkeeping, CRM administration, and technical work command more than general data entry because the skill is verifiable and scarcer.</li>
          <li><strong>Hours and coverage.</strong> Full-time dedicated coverage costs less per hour than small hourly blocks, the same way buying in bulk works anywhere.</li>
          <li><strong>Time zone overlap.</strong> Professionals who work your exact business hours, including overnight shifts their time, cost more than async-only arrangements.</li>
          <li><strong>Who employs them.</strong> If the provider handles contracts, payroll taxes, benefits, and compliance, that infrastructure is inside the price. If not, it is on your books instead.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The hidden costs nobody puts in the quote</h2>
        <p>The hourly rate is the visible cost. These are the ones that decide what you actually spend in a year:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Recruiting time.</strong> Screening 50 applicants, running interviews, and testing finalists commonly takes 15 to 25 hours of your time per hire.</li>
          <li><strong>Failed hires.</strong> A bad freelance hire costs weeks of salary equivalent in rework and restarting the search. Industry estimates put a failed hire at several thousand dollars once lost productivity is counted.</li>
          <li><strong>Turnover.</strong> Freelance virtual assistants commonly serve multiple clients and churn quickly. Every replacement restarts your training investment.</li>
          <li><strong>Management overhead.</strong> Untrained hires need constant instruction. A vetted professional with documented processes needs a weekly check-in instead.</li>
          <li><strong>Tools and access.</strong> Software seats, security provisioning, and equipment policies add up, and someone has to administer them.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>This is why the honest comparison is total cost per year, not rate per hour. Our <a href="../../blog/how-to-hire-remote-employees/">step-by-step hiring guide</a> shows how to structure the process so these costs stay low whichever model you choose.</p>

        <h2>Part-time or full-time: which is cheaper?</h2>
        <p>Part-time hourly support wins when the workload is genuinely small and unpredictable, under about 10 hours a week. Past roughly 15 to 20 hours a week, a dedicated full-time professional is almost always cheaper per hour of output, and you get continuity: the same person, learning your business, every day. Most growing businesses cross that threshold sooner than they expect, which is one of the <a href="../../blog/signs-you-need-a-virtual-assistant/">signs it is time to hire a dedicated remote team</a>.</p>

        <h2>What a dedicated professional costs at Remote Team Partners</h2>
        <p>Our pricing is deliberately simple: one monthly rate per professional, scoped to the role and seniority you need, with recruitment included and no placement fees, ever. Book a discovery call and we will quote the exact figure for your role on the call. We employ the professional, handle payroll and compliance, and include a replacement guarantee on every hire. You can see how the tiers map to roles on our <a href="../../services/admin-operations/">admin and operations</a> and <a href="../../services/accounting-finance/">accounting and finance</a> pages, or <a href="../../contact/">ask us for an exact quote</a> for your role.</p>

        <h2>The bottom line</h2>
        <p>Budget $10 to $30 an hour for a freelancer you manage yourself, $35 to $60 an hour for agency hours, or $1,500 to $2,500 a month for a dedicated full-time professional with the hiring risk handled for you. Pick the model that matches your workload, then judge it on total yearly cost, not the headline rate.</p>
      </article>
